# Read-only systemd status for one exact unit.
# Run this on a Linux host with systemd; replace the full unit when needed.
#
# The runtime asks only for Id, LoadState, ActiveState and SubState through
# `systemctl show`; it does not parse localized/colorized `systemctl status`
# output and it never changes the unit. Masked, failed and not-found states
# remain observations and are not repaired implicitly.
#
# This is the system manager because no `user` profile is written. The full
# `.service` suffix is intentional: an alias or a template is not silently
# converted into another unit. A canonical-Id mismatch is an error.
#
# Bash comparison:
#   systemctl show --no-pager \
#     --property=Id,LoadState,ActiveState,SubState systemd.service
# The yesh form validates the unit and the returned Id before printing it.
service status systemd-journald.service
